A||||4||Red2m4||Electric "Whirring" Sound, Alternator||||1||
Z||000000||Red2m4||05-31-2000||02:48 PM||||There's a rather strange sound coming from the engine of my 2m4.  It seems to be comming from the alternator.  A bad bearing, perhaps?  It drives ok, but I'm afraid it won't for long.  A couple things to remember - <BR>     1.The belts squeal like crazy when ever there is rain/dew around.  <BR>     2. Before the sound occured I accidently<BR>"started" the car while it was running. (Doh!) When I started it again, I noticed the sound about 10 min into my next trip.<P>    I read a message on another board that said you need to remove the oil filter to get the alternaor out from the bottom.  Would it be easier to remove the dog bone,roll the engine forward, and pull the alternator out from above?  Any help you can all offer would be greatly appreciated.<P>                      <BR>PS - Maybe you remember I had a post about a brake fire?  I'll post pic's when I get them!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||ChadMan||05-31-2000||03:46 PM||||"...I loook een my kreestal ball, Red 2M4.  Eet ees your future I see!  You are standing een your Fiero's trunk!  You have a beeg ratchet weeth a looong extension...you are trying to remove the peevot bolt from your alternator...you are cursing!"<P>I had the same symptoms you describe with my 84 Coupe, so I replaced its alternator.  The only problem I ran into was removing the pivot bolt from the alternator.  It was nearly impossible to break loose and was reluctant to come out.  Once I got it free (with lots of Liquid Wrench, a long ratchet, a BFH, and swearing), it came out the bottom easily.  I did remove my oil filter.<P>I weesh you good fortune.||||reg||1
Z||000002||80's BOY||05-31-2000||03:52 PM||||has anyone ever just taken the inner fender well out?? push rivets aren't that complicated to remove. this gives you a nice view of the alternator from underneath, if you are on jackstands. then you will be able to get to the parts easier with more room.||||reg||5
Z||000003||87PegasusGT||05-31-2000||04:28 PM||||Yeah, but they are a @#$%$ to get back in straight.....||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||06-01-2000||09:49 AM||||I just did mine a couple days ago. Put on a 105 amp unit. If it ever has a power shortage now something really bad is happening.<P>Fortunatly I had recently bought a set of long extensions. You need enough extension to go all the way across the engine. The only clear space to turn the wrench is over the tranny.<P>My pivot bolt had a nut on the end of it. I didn't have a hard time with it. (Mine's an 87 so it's a little diferant.) If the older ones don't have a nut on the end use 1/2 drive extensions and handle. These won't windup like long 3/8 ones can. You'll also find this easier if you have a flex joint. then you don't have to worry about having the wrench inline with the bolt.||||reg||1
